Q: How to implement partial function in JavaScript - three-way sum partial application A: <p>The problem with your current implementation of <code>partial</code> is that it is a fixed 2 step process. What you need is a dynamic process.</p>
<p>You can solve that problem with different solutions.</p>
<h2>Specific solution</h2>
<p>If you want an implementation that <em>exactly</em> solves your 4 test cases, all your <code>partial</code> function needs to do is to check the number of given arguments and determine if it should return the result or another function.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It won't work for <code>partialThreeSum()()(1, 2, 3)</code>! If you want that as well, see the general solution.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here's an example:</p>
<pre><code>const partial = (fn, ...initArgs) =&gt; {
  if (initArgs.length &gt;= fn.length) {    // &lt;-- check if enough arguments are given
    return fn(...initArgs);
  }
  return (...restArgs) =&gt; {
    return fn(...initArgs, ...restArgs);
  };
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then the <code>partialThreeSum</code> function can be implemented:</p>
<pre><code>const threeSum = (a, b, c) =&gt; a + b + c;

const partialThreeSum = (a, b, c) =&gt; {
  return partial(threeSum, a, b, c);
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>BUT</strong> this has an implicit problem: When any argument that <code>partialThreeSum</code> expects is missing, <code>undefined</code> is passed to <code>partial</code>. However, because we used the spread operator for <code>...initArgs</code>, the passed <code>undefined</code> counts as given argument and the <code>if (initArgs.length &gt;= fn.length)</code> condition evaluates to <code>true</code>. Bummer.</p>
<p>There's only one way to solve that: We <em>have to</em> use a named function in combination with <code>arguments</code> to evaluate the <em>real</em> number of arguments passed. Let's rewrite <code>partialThreeSum</code>:</p>
<pre><code>function partialThreeSum(a, b, c) {
  return partial.apply(null, [threeSum, ...arguments]);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Let's try it again, the implementation of <code>partialThreeSum</code> should be working right now:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const partial = (fn, ...initArgs) =&gt; {
  if (initArgs.length &gt;= fn.length) {    // &lt;-- check if enough arguments are given
    return fn(...initArgs);
  }
  return (...restArgs) =&gt; {
    return fn(...initArgs, ...restArgs);
  };
}

const threeSum = (a, b, c) =&gt; a + b + c;

function partialThreeSum(a, b, c) {
  return partial.apply(null, [threeSum, ...arguments]);
}

// check partialThreeSum()(1, 2, 3)
console.log(partialThreeSum()(1, 2, 3));

// check partialThreeSum(1, 2)(3)
console.log(partialThreeSum(1, 2)(3));

// check partialThreeSum(1)(2, 3)
console.log(partialThreeSum(1)(2, 3));

// check partialThreeSum(1, 2, 3)
console.log(partialThreeSum(1, 2, 3));</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<h2>General solution</h2>
<p>Another possible way is to use a technique called &quot;auto-currying&quot;, which is a special form of partial application. Usually, a curried function takes one argument at a time per invocation. However, auto currying allows you to pass in all parameters at once <em>or</em> step by step.</p>
<p>This describes a simple and in parts naive implementation of the auto-currying technique, but I hope it helps to understand the principle.</p>
<h3>Helper functions</h3>
<p>Before we can start to implement the <code>curry</code> function, we need a simple helper function here that allows to fix the arity of the returned function (in other terms, we create a function with an exact <code>.length</code> from a variadic function). Let's call it <code>aritize</code>. Here's an example of what it does:</p>
<pre><code>const variadicFunction = (...args) =&gt; { /* does not matter for now */ };

console.log(variadicFunction.length); // -&gt; 0

const binaryFunction = aritize(2, variadicFunction);

console.log(binaryFunction.length); // -&gt; 2
</code></pre>
<p>This is the (naive) implementation: Take a number and a function, return another function with a fixed arity according to the given number and inside it apply the given function to all arguments. Return the result.</p>
<pre><code>function aritize(length, fn) {
  // takes a number and a function and returns a function with an arity that
  // matches the given number. For demo purposes, this only works up to an
  // arity of 3. If you need to support functions with a higher arity, just
  // add appropriate `case`s below (always the same pattern)
  switch (length) {
    case 0:
      return function () { return fn.apply(this, arguments); };
    case 1: 
      return function (a) { return fn.apply(this, arguments); };
    case 2:
      return function (a, b) { return fn.apply(this, arguments); };
    case 3:
      return function (a, b, c) { return fn.apply(this, arguments); };
    default:
      return fn;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<h3>Recursion, recursion, recursion, ...</h3>
<p>OK, now that we have a way to preserve the correct <code>.length</code>, we need to get another - more obvious - problem out of the way: How can we create a function that returns itself if it is either called with too few or no arguments at all, but that returns a result if all arguments are given to it? The answer is: Recursion.</p>
<p>Let's lift this task to another helper function, called <code>curryRec</code>. <code>curryRec</code> takes a function and an array of collected arguments, and it keeps returning functions until enough arguments are collected to execute the function.</p>
<pre><code>function curryRec(fn, args) {
  // takes a function and an array of arguments, returns functions until
  // the given function can be executed
  if (args.length &gt;= fn.length) {
    return fn(...args);
  }
  return aritize(fn.length - args.length, (...restArgs) =&gt; {
    return curryRec(fn, args.concat(restArgs));
  });
}
</code></pre>
<p>Take a closer look here, this should be somewhat familiar to you because it is the implementation of a <code>partial</code> function you've been looking for. 🙂</p>
<p>This is how it works: When <code>curryRec</code> is called, it first checks if <code>fn</code> can execute (and does it if possible, then ends). If not, it returns a function that is ready to take the rest of the arguments list and then calls <code>curryRec</code> again. It is crucial to understand here that we found a way to &quot;freeze&quot; the execution at that point, because the fact that a function is returned here that in turn calls <code>curryRec</code> makes this whole thing not only recursive, but also kind of &quot;async&quot; (not related to <code>async/await</code> or <code>Promise</code>).</p>
<p>And it is also the place where <code>aritize</code> comes into play: We want the function returned from <code>curryRec</code> to always have a <code>.length</code> that reflects the number of <em>missing</em> arguments.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is worth noting that you don't <em>have</em> to solve the recursion with the help of the <code>curryRec</code> function. You can as well get away with an inner function or with default arguments, but IMHO this version is cleaner and easier to understand in that form.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Time to curry!</h3>
<p>Nearly there, I promise. With all of the above out of the way, we can implement a nice and clean <code>curry</code> function. We neatly tuck away the implementation details of <code>curryRec</code>, particularly that it <em>has</em> to be called with an empty <code>[]</code> the first time and we also make sure to do no unecessary work by rejecting to curry functions that have an arity of <code>0</code>.</p>
<pre><code>function curry(fn) {
  if (fn.length &lt; 1) {
    return fn;
  }
  return curryRec(fn, []);
}
</code></pre>
<p>With <code>curry</code> at your disposal, <code>partialThreeSum</code> is easy enough:</p>
<pre><code>const partialThreeSum = curry((a, b, c) =&gt; a + b + c);

// check .length
console.log(partialThreeSum.length);       // -&gt; 3
console.log(partialThreeSum(1).length);    // -&gt; 2
console.log(partialThreeSum(1, 2).length); // -&gt; 1

// check partialThreeSum()(a, b, c)
console.log(partialThreeSum()(1, 2, 3));   // -&gt; 6

// check partialThreeSum(a, b, c)
console.log(partialThreeSum(1, 2, 3));     // -&gt; 6

// check partialThreeSum(a, b)(c)
console.log(partialThreeSum(1, 2)(3));     // -&gt; 6

// check partialThreeSum(a)(b, c)
console.log(partialThreeSum(1)(2, 3));     // -&gt; 6
</code></pre>
<p>If you want to try it, here's a complete stack snippet:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function aritize(length, fn) {
  // takes a number and a function and returns a function with an arity that
  // matches the given number. For demo purposes, this only works up to an
  // arity of 3. If you need to support functions with a higher arity, just
  // add appropriate `case`s below (always the same pattern)
  switch (length) {
    case 0:
      return function () { return fn.apply(this, arguments); };
    case 1: 
      return function (a) { return fn.apply(this, arguments); };
    case 2:
      return function (a, b) { return fn.apply(this, arguments); };
    case 3:
      return function (a, b, c) { return fn.apply(this, arguments); };
    default:
      return fn;
  }
}

function curryRec(fn, args) {
  // takes a function and an array of arguments, returns functions until
  // the given function can be executed
  if (args.length &gt;= fn.length) {
    return fn(...args);
  }
  return aritize(fn.length - args.length, (...restArgs) =&gt; {
    return curryRec(fn, args.concat(restArgs));
  });
}

function curry(fn) {
  if (fn.length &lt; 1) {
    return fn;
  }
  return curryRec(fn, []);
}


// --- TESTS ---

const partialThreeSum = curry((a, b, c) =&gt; a + b + c);

// check .length
console.log(partialThreeSum.length);       // -&gt; 3
console.log(partialThreeSum(1).length);    // -&gt; 2
console.log(partialThreeSum(1, 2).length); // -&gt; 1

// check partialThreeSum()(a, b, c)
console.log(partialThreeSum()(1, 2, 3));   // -&gt; 6

// check partialThreeSum(a, b, c)
console.log(partialThreeSum(1, 2, 3));     // -&gt; 6

// check partialThreeSum(a, b)(c)
console.log(partialThreeSum(1, 2)(3));     // -&gt; 6

// check partialThreeSum(a)(b, c)
console.log(partialThreeSum(1)(2, 3));     // -&gt; 6</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: An exception was thrown while attempting to evaluate the LINQ query parameter expression A: <p>I experienced the same problem.</p>
<p>The problem appeared on code that worked perfectly well before a tooling update and was caused by a C# language <strong>preview</strong> compilation option in the <strong>.csproj</strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
  &lt;LangVersion&gt;preview&lt;/LangVersion&gt;
&lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Actually, the proposition of Arpan is correct, but applying to a large project with many uses of <strong>Contains</strong> is very long, and dangerous (forgotten lines...)</p>
<p>Removing the <strong>preview</strong> option from the csproj fixed the problem without additional danger.</p>
<p>Edit : as says Panagiotis, the best may be to use up to date .Net and C# versions and get rid of the Langage preview information in the project</p>

Q: OneTrust: Check if OneTrust is used in the project and wait for it to have fully finished loading A: <p>I use this way when the code can't be injected before OneTrust script:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>async function getOneTrustReady(complete = true) =&gt; {
  // We use a timeout to test if the condition is true
  // There is no other reliable and easy way to do that. OneTrust use the queue pattern but doesn't use it for this API
  // See https://developer.onetrust.com/onetrust/docs/command-queue and https://developer.onetrust.com/onetrust/docs/embedded-web-forms-methods-and-events#form-loaded they use it for another feature
  while (
    // The full API is available
    // https://developer.onetrust.com/onetrust/docs/javascript-api#on-consent-changed-callback
    typeof Object(window.OneTrust).OnConsentChanged !== 'function' ||
    // The stub already defines a part of the API which is still loading. OnetrustActiveGroups can be read at this step (at the first visit, it's empty, equal to &quot;,,&quot;, which doesn't reflect defaults). Ex: add ?otgeo=FR as query string
    // Note window.OneTrust can be already exist at this step too, but could be incomplete
    (!complete &amp;&amp; !window.OnetrustActiveGroups)
  ) {
    // Wait the next task
    await new Promise((r) =&gt; setTimeout(() =&gt; r()));
  }

  return window.OneTrust;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>await getOneTrustReady(false);
console.log(window.OnetrustActiveGroups.slice(1, -1).split(','));
</code></pre>
<p>or</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>await getOneTrustReady();
console.log(OneTrust.GetDomainData());
</code></pre>
<p>See also <a href="https://developer.onetrust.com/onetrust/docs/ecommerce#:%7E:text=waitForOneTrust" rel="nofollow noreferrer">OneTrust CMP with Shopify Developer Documentation</a></p>
